Activity 1: Internet Reference Models, Internet Protocols, and Domain Name System (DNS)

I learned how computer networks use protocols to communicate and transfer information. Protocols establish rules for sending and receiving data, while the Domain Name System (DNS) translates domain names into IP addresses. Together, these systems help users access websites and online services. This knowledge will support my web design studies by helping me understand how websites connect to users through the Internet.

Activity 2: Review IP Address Settings for Your Local Network

I learned that IP addresses identify devices on a network and allow them to communicate. Network settings also include information about the default gateway, which connects a local network to other networks. Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) can automatically assign IP addresses to devices. In the future, this knowledge will help me understand network configurations and troubleshoot connection problems when accessing online resources for school or work.

Activity 3: Verify Internet Connectivity

I learned that several tools can help identify network connection problems. The ping command checks whether a device can communicate with another address, while traceroute shows the route data takes toward a destination. DNS lookup tools can also determine whether a domain name resolves to an IP address. I can apply these concepts when troubleshooting Internet problems, especially if a website won't load or my connection becomes unreliable while I'm completing assignments.

Activity 4: Research and Identify the Components Required to Set Up a Home Network

I learned that a home network requires appropriate equipment, such as a modem, router, and devices that connect through wired or wireless connections. A router directs data between networks, while a wireless access point allows devices to connect through Wi-Fi. I also learned that network security is important for protecting connected devices and personal information. I can apply this knowledge when setting up a home network, choosing reliable equipment, and securing my connection for college assignments and future web design projects.

Overall, these activities helped me understand how network devices, protocols, and troubleshooting tools work together to support Internet communication. This knowledge will be useful as I continue learning about technology and developing my web design skills.
